{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs",
  "item": "impl[CachedParse/SourceProvider]/fn[read]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This required SourceProvider callback is an error guard: the fixture\n supplies cached snapshots by overriding parse. Structural search must\n use those snapshots without refetching. Calling read would fail the\n contract tested below; primitive reads are exercised by other providers."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#impl[CachedParse/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` is justified. It sits on an explicit function with a body, and its fenced commentary gives a substantive reason, not just difficulty. The commentary agrees with the current source:\n\n- `read` is a required `SourceProvider` callback. It always returns `SourceError::Invalid(\"cached parser must not refetch\")`, so it is an error guard.\n- `CachedParse` overrides `parse` (`impl[CachedParse/SourceProvider]/fn[parse]`). That override builds a snapshot from fixed in-memory bytes and never calls `read`.\n- The test `structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope` runs structural search with `.unwrap()` on the result. It asserts the `parses` counter is 3 and that matches come from the cached snapshot. If search called `read`, the test would fail. So `read` should never run, and zero execution is expected.\n\nThe commentary also says primitive reads are exercised by other providers. That is a plausible reason for not covering `read` here, and no part of the commentary contradicts the source.",
